In a fresh Continual-ARC instance labeled `task-4f5f6fe0`, a stored note described diagonal rays from a different geometry. The current instance had a 20×20 test with a sparse lower-left motif, while its demos varied substantially: one showed diagonal zero extensions from a local pattern, another was an unchanged compact motif, and another was an unchanged two-row motif. None provided evidence for the remembered test ray rule. The assistant nevertheless transferred that ray idea and submitted a speculative diagonal 7 extension; it was rejected.

Lesson: recurring IDs and superficial ray/motif resemblance are retrieval cues, not transformation evidence. Keep the stored rule as a hypothesis, then replay its abstract details against every current input/output pair: grid geometry, endpoints, direction, color, source retention, and boundary termination. A candidate must exactly explain all demos and materially constrain the test. If demos conflict or are identity examples, reopen task class and representation rather than changing a guessed ray's length/color. Request further evidence only for a named ambiguity whose resolution could change the candidate.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-4f5f6fe0-reference-transfer-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/session-task-4f5f6fe0-review.md", "content": "# Recurrent task reference-transfer failure

This instance reused task ID `task-4f5f6fe0` but had a different geometry from the remembered ray-extension example. A prior reference described a diagonal continuation rule for a 14×25 scene; the current test was 23×6, and the available demo was 26×24 with a diagonal ray from a motif endpoint. Treating the reference as ground truth led to multiple unsupported test submissions: first a single-cell recoloring, then diagonal extensions, and then retries that adjusted local placements/colors without evidence. The second demo did show a diagonal ray (color 6) continuing from a compact motif toward the top-left boundary, but it did not validate the assumed test transformation.

Reusable lesson: same task ID and superficially related rays are not proof of a shared rule. Before transferring a session reference, replay its abstract rule against every current demo, including direction, source endpoint, marker/template roles, retained cells, color behavior, dimensions, and boundary termination. If the rule fails exact replay or does not constrain the test, treat the test as unresolved. After incorrect feedback, avoid cosmetic variants; request a demo only for a concrete ambiguity or reassess representation/task class. This transcript did not establish a validated test solution.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/session-task-4f5f6fe0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md", "content": "# Resetting after failed ARC hypotheses

Repeated incorrect outputs indicate the task class or answer representation may be wrong, not merely a coordinate detail. Before spending another attempt, revisit all demonstrations: list input/output dimensions, identify separators and partitions, enumerate each region's candidate features, and test a compact region-summary mapping across every pair. Do not submit a revised pattern unless it exactly reproduces all known demonstrations. A new demo request should target a clearly stated unresolved distinction; serial requests without analysis impose cost without improving inference.

For small outputs from large partitioned canvases, prioritize understanding the relationship between each cell/region and the compact answer. Keep a candidate mapping table and explicitly account for exceptions (e.g. output cells retaining a background/category color). Only after the mapping reproduces all demos should it be applied to the test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/marker-anchor-offsets.md", "content": "# Marker-to-template anchor offsets

When demos show sparse markers receiving copies of an embedded motif, keep two coordinate concepts separate: (1) the motif's bounding-box origin and (2) the motif coordinate that aligns with a marker. Represent the latter as a signed offset from the origin and validate it against multiple marker/copy pairs. A plausible colored pattern is not enough: replay every marker footprint against the complete output, including the original motif, overlaps, clipping, and unchanged candidate markers.

A repeated failure mode is to shift the right motif by an intuitively appealing amount (top-left, center, a nearby marker, or a visually salient source pixel) without validating the anchor. On incorrect feedback, discard that placement hypothesis and derive the offset anew from observed coordinate correspondences. Also validate serialization independently: every row must have the expected width and all values must be legal before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/marker-anchor-offsets.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/speculative-submission-after-demo.md", "content": "# Avoiding speculative submission after a demo

A returned demonstration pair is evidence to analyze, not permission to extrapolate from a salient resemblance. Record the exact changed and unchanged cells, then check whether the rule is uniquely supported for the current test. If an assumed stamp, anchor, orientation, or source-retention rule is not established by the pair, do not submit a guessed grid. Name at least two plausible hypotheses and request another pair only if their predictions differ in a way that can change the test answer. Analyze every newly returned pair before acting again. This is especially important after a failed submission: changing only one guessed coordinate or preserving a different portion of the candidate is not meaningful recovery unless new evidence supports it.
